  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000120122,
      "SummaryText": "U.S. Amines is dedicated to being an industry leader in protecting the environment, the health, and the safety of both our employees and surrounding communities. Like our local community and industrial neighbors, U.S. Amines' fifty site employees want a clean, safe environment with no illnesses or injuries at work and home. That is why U.S. Amines has a \"zero-incidents\" goal. The zero-incidents goal is a commitment to reducing emissions and having zero injuries and zero environmental incidents. \n\nThe U.S. Amines Bucks' Plant participates in a local community advisory panel and works with other area industrial facilities to promote safety in operations. The Lemoyne Community Advisory Panel was one of the first community advisory panels established in the United States. The Lemoyne Industrial Park companies have been recognized by local, state, and national organizations for outstanding cooperation. This continued partnership ensures the community is a safe place now and in the future. \n\nU.S. Amines manufactures several different chemicals called alkyl amines. These alkyl amines go into the production of agricultural chemicals, water treatment chemicals, and pharmaceutical compounds. For example, the product isopropylamine is a combination of isopropyl alcohol and ammonia, and isopropylamine gets sold to other chemical facilities that produce herbicides. Regulated substances held at the stationary source, U.S. Amines, are as follows: toxic substances, ammonia, propionitrile, and cyclohexylamine; flammable substances, ethylamine and isopropylamine. \n\nThe general accidental release prevention program actions include, but are not limited to, transfer operations being visually monitored; tanks, valves, hoses, pumps, piping, and safety devices being properly maintained and kept in good operating condition; commercial carriers following correct procedures when unloading or loading, and remaining with their equipment at all times. \n\nAccidental release prevention policies at"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000120122,
      "SummaryText": "the stationary source, including but not limited to, are monitoring the security of the facility with a fence and security post; equipping hazardous material storage tanks that have drain valves with a blind flange; maintaining adequate facility lighting; designing loading/unloading areas so that drainage flows into a containment tank or basin; preventing premature departure; checking for leaks prior to filling tank trucks or cars and departure; constructing tanks out of chemical-compatible materials; designing storage dikes to hold 110% of its tank's contents; and maintaining mechanical integrity of equipment. These are policies outlined in a manual available plant-wide.\n\nChemical-specific accidental release prevention program actions for regulated substances include dikes and pans. The ammonia storage tanks do not use dikes due to evaporation. Isopropylamine, ethylamine, cyclohexylamine, and propionitrile storage tanks do make use of dikes. All storage tanks of the chemicals are routinely inspected to maintain the mechanical integrity. Ammonia, ethylamine, and isopropylamine are all readily soluble in water, which would help with mitigating an accidental release.\n\nIf there is an accidental release, the priority is to evaluate the safety of plant personnel and to determine if outside assistance is needed. Then, the containment procedures and incident reporting procedures are followed. If the substance is flammable, foam or other suitable substances are to be applied to the released substance to prevent ignition and aid in vapor control. All storm and sanitary drains are covered to prevent the released material from flowing offsite. The containment area is barricaded and secured to prevent entry and possible injury. A site-specific incident report is completed, and if needed, an incident report for regulatory agencies is prepared.\n\nThe emergency response policy for the stationary source is detailed in the Emergency Response Manual. This includes procedures for notif"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 2,
      "FacilityID": 1000120122,
      "SummaryText": "ication and communication, categorizing emergencies, evacuations, mutual aid resources, incident management, site control, personnel protection and safety, decontamination, emergency medical care, hazardous material release, fire emergencies, rescue emergencies, off-site response, severe weather, and bomb threats. Pre-incident planning and post incident considerations are also included in the program. \n\nThe emergency response program follows the Incident Management System for emergency operations and includes the training of all production personnel to be members of the Bucks Emergency Services Team (BEST). The training includes firefighting, hazardous materials response, confined space rescue, first aid and CPR. Mutual aid agreements with other local industrial sites and local fire and emergency response agencies complement the emergency response plan. The plant conducts weekly tests of the plant alarm system and conducts emergency response drills with local industrial facilities, local fire departments, and the county emergency response team, Mobile County Emergency Management Agency (EMA). \n\nThe U.S. Amines Bucks' plant complies with RMP and OSHA 1910.119, (the OSHA Process Safety Regulation) through the consolidated Process Safety Management Program. The Bucks' plant manages all plant processes per the site's Process Safety Management Program. The program meets all the RMP and OSHA requirements such as\n\n\u0095\tDevelop and implement a risk management system\n\u0095\tConduct hazard assessments of all processes\n\u0095\tImplement accident prevention requirements\n\u0095\tDevelop an emergency response program that is coordinated with local agencies\n\u0095\tTrack and analyze incidents to prevent recurrence\n\nAlso, U.S. Amines requires all plant employees to attend at least one safety meeting a month. Safety meetings include training on regulatory requirements and plant specific issues. Testing is conducted in safety meetings to ensure information is understood. The plant conducts monthly self-paced"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 3,
      "FacilityID": 1000120122,
      "SummaryText": "computer-based training on regulatory safety topics. The computer-based training includes testing, as well. There have not been any accidents in the past five years.\n\nPlanned changes to improve safety include the implementation of a hazard recognition process that will support safe work practices by enhancing our understanding of how to manage and prevent energy sources that create hazards.  Identifying hazards is a fundamental aspect of keeping employees, contractors and the community safe by understanding how to better recognize potential hazards in the workplace and how to better control and mitigate energy sources that would be identified as a hazard."
    }
  ]
}